## Migration: EventProcessor to .NET 8

### Problems in the Original Code

| API | Issue on .NET 8 |
|-----|-----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable on `WaitOne` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware |
| Race condition | Multiple enqueues with one `Reset()` can lose signals |

### Patterns Identified

1. **Cancellable work loop** → `CancellationToken` checked in loop
2. **Blocking call interruption** → `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle`

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Blocks until signaled OR cancelled
            int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);
            if (index == 1) // cancellation token fired
                break;

            // Drain all queued items (fixes original race condition)
            while (true)
            {
                string? item;
                lock (_lock)
                {
                    if (_queue.Count == 0)
                        break;
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
                }
                Handle(item);
            }

            _newEvent.Reset();
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();             // wakes WaitAny immediately
        _thread?.Join(5000);       // wait for graceful exit
        // No fallback — cooperative cancellation means the loop exits
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|-----------|
| `WaitOne()` ignores cancellation | Use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` as second handle |
| Cancellation isn't immediate if `Handle()` is slow | Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `Handle` if it's long-running |
| Original code loses items (one signal per `WaitOne`) | Drain the full queue after each wake-up |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Implement `IDisposable` |
| No forced kill available | If `Handle()` can hang indefinitely on uncooperative code, move it to a child process |

---

### Even Simpler: Use `BlockingCollection<T>`

If you don't need the manual threading, .NET 8 provides a built-in producer/consumer:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
                Handle(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _thread?.Join(5000);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _queue.Dispose();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

`BlockingCollection` + `GetConsumingEnumerable(token)` handles all the signaling, locking, and cancellation internally — eliminating the manual `WaitHandle`/lock/queue coordination entirely.
